@Chapter How to write a recognition method

This chapter explains how to add a new recognition method to the
<Package>recog</Package> package. Chapter <Ref Chap="recognition"/> documents
the attributes of recognition nodes used here, Chapter <Ref Chap="methsel"/>
the method objects and method databases. Feedback on this chapter is welcome,
see Section <Ref Sect="feedback"/>.

@Section Overview of group recognition
@SectionLabel HowToOverview

Let $G = \langle X \rangle$ be a group, for example a permutation group or a
matrix group over a finite field. <Package>recog</Package> solves the
constructive membership problem for $G$: given an element $g$ of a group
containing $G$, decide whether $g \in G$, and if so, return a straight line
program (SLP) that computes $g$ from $X$. To this end it chooses
<E>nice generators</E> $Y$ of $G$ and an SLP from $X$ to $Y$; an SLP for $g$
in terms of $Y$, composed with it, yields an SLP in terms of $X$.

The work is organised recursively along a <E>recognition tree</E>, also called
composition tree. Each node is a recognition node (see Section
<Ref Sect="rirecord"/>) for a group $G$ and is of one of two kinds:
- a <E>leaf node</E>, for which constructive membership is solved directly;
  typically $G$ is (almost) simple, possibly modulo scalars, or otherwise
  easy, for example trivial, cyclic, or small enough for a stabiliser chain;
- a <E>non-leaf node</E>, for which a homomorphism
  $\varphi \colon G \to H$ was found; its children recognise the image $H$
  and the kernel $N = \ker(\varphi)$.

$G$ is <E>constructively recognised</E> once its isomorphism type is known,
for example $M_{11}$ or $PSL_2(9)$, and constructive membership is solved as
above. Section <Ref Sect="techrecog"/> explains how
<Ref Func="RecogniseGeneric"/> builds the tree and assembles the SLPs of a
non-leaf node from those of image and kernel. For background see
<Cite Key="BHLGO15"/> and <Cite Key="NS06"/>.

@Subsection Terminology

A <E>recognition method</E> is an object in the filter
<Ref Filt="IsRecogMethod"/>: a &GAP; function together with a unique name,
its <E>stamp</E>, and a comment. Chapter <Ref Chap="recognition"/> calls
recognition methods <Q>find homomorphism methods</Q>. They are not &GAP;
methods installed with <Ref Func="InstallMethod" BookName="Ref"/>, and the
method selection of Chapter <Ref Chap="methsel"/> decides which of them is
tried when.

A successful recognition method either makes its node a leaf or provides a
homomorphism; we call it a <E>leaf method</E> or a <E>non-leaf method</E>
accordingly. The distinction concerns a single call: nothing prevents a method
from doing either, depending on its input.

@Section Projective groups
@SectionLabel HowToProjective

<Package>recog</Package> represents a projective group by a matrix group
$G \leq GL(d,q)$ and recognises $G/Z$, where $Z$ is the group of scalar
matrices in $G$. Two matrices represent the same element of $G/Z$ if they
differ by a scalar factor. A matrix group which none of the few matrix
methods handles reaches the method <Ref Subsect="GoProjective" Style="Text"/>,
whose image is the same group treated projectively and whose kernel is $Z$.
Hence most methods are installed for projective groups, see Section
<Ref Sect="projmethods"/>.

The component `ri!.projective` of a projective node is `true`. Code that runs
at projective nodes
- compares elements with <Ref Attr="isone"/> and <Ref Attr="isequal"/>, and
  computes element orders with <Ref Attr="OrderFunc"/>, instead of `IsOne`,
  `=` and `Order`;
- must use homomorphisms which are well defined modulo scalars: matrices that
  differ by a scalar factor must have images that represent the same element;
- may return from its `slpforelement` function an SLP that evaluates to the
  given element up to a scalar factor;
- must set the size of a leaf to $|G/Z|$, see Section <Ref Sect="Section_HowToLeaf"/>.

@Section Input and return values
@SectionLabel HowToInput

A recognition method takes one argument, the recognition node `ri`. It obtains
the group to recognise with <Ref Attr="Grp"/>, and on success stores its
results in `ri`; Sections <Ref Sect="Section_HowToLeaf"/> and
<Ref Sect="Section_HowToNonLeaf"/> list what it has to store.
<Package>recog</Package> asks for the minimal information and derives the
rest.

A recognition method returns one of four values. Section
<Ref Sect="howcalled"/> explains how <Ref Func="CallMethods"/> reacts to them.
- `Success`: the method has done its work; no other method is tried.
- `NeverApplicable`: the method cannot work for this group, for example
  because it handles only transitive groups. It is not called again for this
  node. A method should find this out quickly, without big computations.
- `TemporaryFailure`: the method failed but may succeed when called again.
  This is typical for a Las Vegas algorithm with unlucky random choices; for
  example <Ref Subsect="Giant" Style="Text"/> returns it when it found no
  evidence that the group is a giant.
- `NotEnoughInformation`: the method needs information which is not known
  yet, but which other methods may find. For example
  <Ref Subsect="Imprimitive" Style="Text"/> returns it as long as it is not
  known whether the group is transitive.

A method that does not succeed may still store what it learnt, in `ri` or in
the group, so that other methods can use it. For example
<Ref Subsect="Imprimitive" Style="Text"/> calls `SetIsPrimitive(G, true)`
before returning `NeverApplicable`.

The function <Ref Func="BindRecogMethod"/> creates a recognition method and
stores it, under its stamp, in one of the records
<Ref Var="FindHomMethodsGeneric"/>, <Ref Var="FindHomMethodsPerm"/>,
<Ref Var="FindHomMethodsMatrix"/> and <Ref Var="FindHomMethodsProjective"/>.
Its arguments are the name of the record, the stamp, the comment, an optional
record of options, and the function; the examples below show its use. The
stamp must be unique: it names the method in the method tables of Chapter
<Ref Chap="methods"/>, in the display of a recognition tree, and in the record
of <Ref Func="CallMethods"/>. The only option,
`validatesOrAlwaysValidInput`, declares that the method checks whether its
input is valid, or works for every input; currently it is not used.

@Section Leaf methods
@SectionLabel HowToLeaf

A leaf method must
- mark the node as a leaf with `SetFilterObj(ri, IsLeaf)`, see
  <Ref Filt="IsLeaf"/>;
- set <Ref Attr="slpforelement"/> to a function which takes `ri` and an
  element $g$, and returns an SLP for $g$ in terms of the nice generators $Y$,
  or `fail` if $g \notin G$;
- define the nice generators: either set <Ref Attr="slptonice"/> to an SLP
  from the original generators $X$ to $Y$, or set <Ref Attr="NiceGens"/>,
  or do neither, in which case $Y = X$;
- set the order of the group with `SetSize(ri, n)` if the node is projective.
  Otherwise doing so is optional, but recommended: by default
  <Package>recog</Package> computes `Size(Grp(ri))`, which may be expensive.

<Ref Func="RecogniseGeneric"/> derives the remaining data, for example
<Ref Attr="NiceGens"/> from <Ref Attr="slptonice"/>. If the method sets
neither <Ref Attr="StdPresentation"/> nor <Ref Attr="CalcStdPresentation"/>,
a generic and expensive function computes a presentation when the tree is
verified. <Ref Func="RecogniseGeneric"/> marks every leaf as correct (see
<Ref Prop="IsCorrect"/>), so a leaf method must not return a wrong result.

The following method recognises trivial groups. It is the base case of the
recursion and works for permutation, matrix and projective groups.

@InsertCode FindHomMethodsGeneric.TrivialGroup

The method tests the generators with `ri!.isone`, the value of
<Ref Attr="isone"/>, so that it also works for projective groups. Its
`slptonice` returns one element, the first generator to the power $0$: the
only nice generator is the identity. The function stored in `slpforelement` is
the following.

@InsertCode SLPforElementFuncsGeneric.TrivialGroup

It returns `fail` if `g` is not the identity, and otherwise an SLP with one
input, the nice generator, that returns the identity. Such functions are
stored in the records <Ref Var="SLPforElementFuncsGeneric"/>,
<Ref Var="SLPforElementFuncsPerm"/>, <Ref Var="SLPforElementFuncsMatrix"/>
and <Ref Var="SLPforElementFuncsProjective"/>.

The method <Ref Subsect="Giant" Style="Text"/> is a leaf method of realistic
size. It computes its nice generators as elements with memory (see Section
<Ref Sect="Section_HowToMemory"/>), obtains `slptonice` from them with
<Ref Func="SLPOfElms" BookName="Ref"/>, and sets the size and a presentation.

@Section Installing a method
@SectionLabel HowToInstall

To let <Package>recog</Package> use a method, add it to a method database
with <Ref Func="AddMethod"/>:

@InsertCode AddMethod_Perm_FindHomMethodsGeneric.TrivialGroup

The arguments are the database, the method, and its rank. The databases
<Ref Var="FindHomDbPerm"/>, <Ref Var="FindHomDbMatrix"/> and
<Ref Var="FindHomDbProjective"/> hold the methods for permutation, matrix and
projective groups. A method can be in several databases, with different
ranks; the trivial group method is also installed by

@InsertCode AddMethod_Matrix_FindHomMethodsGeneric.TrivialGroup
@InsertCode AddMethod_Projective_FindHomMethodsGeneric.TrivialGroup

Methods with higher rank are tried first. The right rank depends on the other
methods in the database, which the tables in Chapter <Ref Chap="methods"/>
list. As a rule of thumb, methods which are fast or likely to apply should be
tried before slow methods or methods which rarely apply. The ranks in a
database must be distinct, also from those of the hints, see Section
<Ref Sect="Section_HowToNonLeaf"/>.

Document the method in a chunk named after its stamp, next to its definition:

```@listing
#! @BeginChunk MyStamp
#! Describe what the method does, and when it returns which value.
#! @EndChunk
```

Building the manual with `makedoc.g` then adds the method to Chapter
<Ref Chap="methods"/>, with its rank, its comment, and the place of its
definition.

@Section Non-leaf methods
@SectionLabel HowToNonLeaf

A non-leaf method finds a homomorphism $\varphi \colon G \to H$ and stores it
with `SetHomom(ri, hom)`, see <Ref Attr="Homom"/>. Then
<Ref Func="RecogniseGeneric"/> recognises the image $H$, computes generators
of the kernel $N$, recognises $N$, and derives the nice generators and the
`slpforelement` function of the node. Section <Ref Sect="findhomo"/> lists
the duties of such a method; the most common ones are:
- If `hom` cannot handle every element of the group containing $G$, set
  <Ref Attr="validatehomominput"/> to a function that rejects the elements
  outside its source. Otherwise membership tests may give wrong answers or
  run into errors.
- If the image needs other methods than $G$, for example because a matrix
  group is mapped to a permutation group, set <Ref Attr="methodsforimage"/>
  to the appropriate database.
- By default the kernel is generated by the normal closure of a few random
  kernel elements, see <Ref Attr="findgensNmeth"/>. If `hom` is injective,
  set `findgensNmeth(ri).method := FindKernelDoNothing`. Kernel elements
  which the method finds can be appended to <Ref Attr="gensN"/>; they need a
  memory, see Section <Ref Sect="Section_HowToMemory"/>. If random elements
  may generate a proper subgroup of the kernel, set
  <Ref Attr="immediateverification"/> to `true`.

The following method restricts a permutation group to an orbit.

@InsertCode FindHomMethodsPerm.NonTransitive
@InsertCode AddMethod_Perm_FindHomMethodsPerm.NonTransitive

The functions `Orb` and `OrbActionHomomorphism` come from the
<Package>orb</Package> package.

@Subsection Hints for image and kernel

A method often learns something that helps to recognise the image or the
kernel. It can store such knowledge in the records
<Ref Attr="InitialDataForImageRecogNode"/> and
<Ref Attr="InitialDataForKernelRecogNode"/>, whose components are copied into
the node for the image or the kernel, respectively. Their component `hints`
is a method database whose methods are tried together with those of the
database of the new node, ordered by rank; see Section
<Ref Sect="hintsystem"/>.

For example <Ref Subsect="ReducibleIso" Style="Text"/> conjugates a reducible
matrix group into block lower triangular form, stores the blocks in the
component `blocks` of `InitialDataForImageRecogNode(ri)`, and adds the
following method to its `hints` with rank 4000, so that the image tries it
first.

@InsertCode FindHomMethodsMatrix.BlockLowerTriangular

The method maps each matrix to its block diagonal part. It computes the images
of the generators first, as the image group is needed to define `hom`. The
image gets the hint <Ref Subsect="BlockDiagonal" Style="Text"/>, the kernel,
a $p$-group of block lower unitriangular matrices, gets the hint
<Ref Subsect="LowerLeftPGroup" Style="Text"/>, and
`RECOG.FindKernelLowerLeftPGroup` computes kernel generators instead of the
default normal closure. The function `GroupHomByFuncWithData` comes from the
<Package>orb</Package> package.

@Section Elements with memory
@SectionLabel HowToMemory

An element with memory remembers how it was computed from a list of
generators, and <Ref Func="SLPOfElms" BookName="Ref"/> turns this into an
SLP; see Section <Ref Sect="Objects with Memory" BookName="Ref"/>. Methods use
such elements to obtain `slptonice` and kernel generators.

Elements with memory can be multiplied, or passed together to
<Ref Func="SLPOfElms" BookName="Ref"/>, only if they stem from the same call
of <Ref Func="GeneratorsWithMemory" BookName="Ref"/>. The generators of
`Grp(ri)` have no memory; the component `ri!.gensHmem` holds them with memory.
A method that needs elements with memory therefore uses `ri!.gensHmem`, for
example by working in `Group(ri!.gensHmem)`, and not
`GroupWithMemory(Grp(ri))`, which starts a new, incompatible memory. In
particular, elements appended to <Ref Attr="gensN"/> must stem from
`ri!.gensHmem`, because <Ref Func="RecogniseGeneric"/> computes
<Ref Attr="gensNslp"/> from them with
<Ref Func="SLPOfElms" BookName="Ref"/>.

For random elements, call `RandomElm(ri, stamp, true).el`, which returns an
element with memory derived from `ri!.gensHmem`, or
`RandomElm(ri, stamp, false).el` for one without memory. The $i$-th call with
one stamp returns the same element as the $i$-th call with any other stamp, so
methods tried at the same node share their random elements instead of
computing new ones. Use
<Ref Func="StripMemory" BookName="Ref"/> to remove the memory, for example
before storing nice generators.

@Section Testing a method
@SectionLabel HowToTest

<Ref Func="TryFindHomMethod"/> runs a single method on a group, without
method selection and recursion. To test a method in context, recognise groups
with <Ref Func="RecogniseGroup"/> and view the result; the display shows the
stamp of the method that succeeded at each node. The function
`RECOG.TestGroup(G, projective, size)` recognises $G$, checks its size, and
checks <Ref Func="SLPforElement"/> on random elements inside and outside $G$.
The test files in `tst/working` use it; `tst/testquick.g` runs the quick ones.
